@h1 Comments h1@

A comment is a note to yourself that never reaches the reader. Wit has two
forms: a line comment that runs to the end of a line, and a block comment that
can sit inline or span many lines. Both are stripped before any output —
whether you render to HTML, Markdown, or PDF, a comment is simply gone.

@h2 Why keep notes in the source h2@

Drafting is thinking. You want to leave a "verify this date", a "cut this if the
section runs long", a reminder of where an argument is heading — right next to
the prose it concerns, without it ever showing up in the finished document.
Because comments live in the text file, they travel with your manuscript in git
and diff like everything else.

@h2 The line comment h2@

A tilde followed by a space starts a line comment. Everything from the tilde to
the end of that line disappears:

@@@pre
~ remember to verify the commissioning year against the county records

The lighthouse was commissioned in 1847.
pre@@@

Only the paragraph survives; the note is gone. The space after the tilde is
required — a tilde glued straight to a word is _not_ a comment (more on that
below).

@h2 The block comment h2@

Two tildes open a block comment; a closing fence ends it. It can vanish in the
middle of a sentence:

@@@pre
The bell ~~ TODO: confirm the exact year ~~/ rang on the hour.
pre@@@

renders as "The bell  rang on the hour." — the aside between the fences is
removed. The same fences can wrap many lines at once:

@@@pre
~~
Draft note: this whole passage may move to chapter two.
Ask the editor before cutting it.
~~/
The keeper climbed the stair as he always had.
pre@@@

Everything between the open and close fences is dropped; the paragraph after it
is untouched.

@h2 Every rule, in one place h2@

@ul
@li _A line comment needs the space._ A tilde-then-space at the start of a line
opens a line comment to end-of-line. Without the space it is ordinary prose. li@
@li _A block comment opens anywhere._ The doubled-tilde fence opens a comment
even at the start of a line, and only the exact closing fence ends it. li@
@li _A bare doubled tilde inside an open block is content._ Because only the
closing fence ends the comment, you can use a doubled tilde as a divider inside a
long block comment without closing it early. li@
@li _Shell paths inside a comment are safe._ A home path such as
@code ~/Documents/notes code@ or @code ~/.bashrc code@ sitting inside a block
comment does not close it — only the exact closing fence does. li@
@li _An empty comment is legal._ An open fence immediately followed by a close
fence renders nothing at all. li@
ul@

@h2 The tilde in ordinary prose h2@

Outside the two comment forms, a tilde is just a character. Glued to a token it
never starts a comment, so approximations and paths read normally:

@@@pre
It took ~6 hours. The file is in ~/Documents. We wrote x ~ y for "about equal".
pre@@@

All three are plain prose. Only a leading tilde-plus-space (a line comment) or a
doubled tilde (a block comment) is special.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _A standalone note glues its neighbours together._ A line comment sitting
between two prose lines with no blank lines around it consumes its own line _and_
the newlines on either side, so the two paragraphs run together with no space
between them. The fix is simple: leave a blank line on each side of a standalone
note. li@
@li _A comment as the first line inside a container collapses the next block._
If a comment is the very first thing inside a node body — the opening line of a
@@@code @section code@@@ or a grid — the block right after it can be wrongly folded
into a paragraph. Always put a blank line after a comment that leads a container
body. li@
@li _Forgetting the space after the tilde._ A tilde pressed against a word is not
a line comment; it is prose punctuation. Write the tilde, then a space, then your
note. li@
ul@

@aside(class note) 💡 The blank-line habit is worth building: a blank line before
and after any standalone comment keeps both of the mistakes above from ever
happening. aside@

